<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth was a federated state created by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-lublin">Union of Lublin</a> in 1569, joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-poland">Kingdom of Poland</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a>. It was one of the largest states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, notable for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elective-monarchy">elected monarchy</a>, powerful noble parliament and relative <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a>. Weakened by internal paralysis and foreign intervention, it was partitioned out of existence by its neighbours by 1795.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth.jpg" alt="Polish–Lithuanian Commonwealth" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olek Remesz (wiki-pl: Orem, commons: Orem) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chor%C4%85giew_kr%C3%B3lewska_kr%C3%B3la_Zygmunta_III_Wazy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
